  "id": "lateral-band-walk",
  "name": "Lateral Band Walk",
  "definition": "Resisted lateral stepping with a loop immediately above the ankles, a self-selected squat posture, and approximately thirty-centimeter initial foot spacing and lead steps. A repetition consists of a leading step followed by a trailing step restoring that gap; repeat equally in the opposite direction. The lead hip abducts against the loop and the trailing hip adducts under band tension with eccentric abductor control. Berry directly recorded gluteus medius, gluteus maximus and TFL in moving and stance limbs; primary gluteus medius and secondary TFL are categorical emphasis judgments, not EMG rankings. Gluteus maximus, vasti, abs, obliques and soleus receive conservative postural control roles for the maintained squat, trunk and stance. No deliberate hip rotation or concentric adductor role is inferred. Upright walking, bands above the knees or around the feet, diagonal monster walks, crossover steps, squatting on every step, and unsupported open-chain abduction remain outside this family. Isolation describes the defining resisted hip motion; the held squat and foot-clearance adjustments are nondefining. Both moving and stance hip abductors contribute as support shifts and the pelvis travels.",
